# Database Schema

Use `driver_days` (not `check_ins`) — a day record is more than arrival.

## Core Tables

### `profiles` (id = auth.users.id)
`role`: admin | driver · `status`: pending | approved | rejected | suspended · first_name, last_name, phone, email, profile_photo

### `driver_documents`
`document_type`: id | pdp · `file_path` (Storage path, not file bytes) · `status`: pending | approved | rejected

### `vehicles` + `driver_vehicle`
Create vehicles now even if V1 is light. `driver_vehicle` tracks assignment history with start_date/end_date.

### `driver_days` (core MVP table)
```
driver_id, vehicle_id, date, started_at, ended_at,
starting_odometer, ending_odometer, total_earnings, notes, status
```

### `expenses` (linked to driver_days)
```
driver_day_id, driver_id, vehicle_id, type, amount, description, receipt_path
```
Types: `fuel` | `maintenance` | `repair` | `other`

## Storage Paths (private buckets)

```
driver-documents/{driver_id}/id.pdf
driver-documents/{driver_id}/pdp.pdf
driver-profile-images/{driver_id}/profile.jpg
expense-receipts/{driver_id}/{expense_id}.jpg
```

Never store document bytes in PostgreSQL. Use signed URLs for private buckets.

## Example Daily Record

```
Thabo — 29 Aug: Earnings R1,850 | Fuel R350 | Maintenance R1,200 | Net R300
```
